# pyenv
#   For a library or package, you might want to ignore these files since the code is
#   intended to run in multiple environments; otherwise, check them in:
# .python-version

# pipenv
#   According to pypa/pipenv#598, it is recommended to include Pipfile.lock in version control.
#   However, in case of collaboration, if having platform-specific dependencies or dependencies
#   having no cross-platform support, pipenv may install dependencies that don't work, or not
#   install all needed dependencies.
#Pipfile.lock

# poetry
#   Similar to Pipfile.lock, poetry.lock contains the exact dependency versions.
#   It is typically recommended to commit it, but if you don't want to:
#poetry.lock

# pdm
#   Similar to Pipfile.lock, pdm.lock contains the exact dependency versions.
#pdm.lock

# virtualenv
.venv/
venv/
ENV/
env/
#   For a library or package, you might want to ignore virtualenv since it is environment-specific:
#   But for a deployed application, you might want to pin it or not.

